Round 2 on branch codex/sandbox-limits (you are in its worktree, which already holds round 1 at 2c42c7a). Fix the 3 BLOCKING findings of the Codex review ops/codex/review-20261105T175836.md. It may be missing from this worktree, so here they are:
1. Detached children escape. A child that clears its env, calls setsid() and is reparented between polls is neither tracked nor killed, and cleanup reports ok. Fix: make the runner process that launches each sandbox task a child subreaper (prctl(PR_SET_CHILD_SUBREAPER=36, 1) via ctypes, Linux only, unprivileged), so orphans reparent to it and stay discoverable as its descendants whatever their env, pgid or sid. Discovery = descendants of the runner, minus the runner's own non-task threads/helpers, scoped to the task (track task roots; anything reparented to the runner while a task runs belongs to the running task, since tasks are sequential per runner process; if GOLD_CONCURRENCY>1 uses threads in one process, use one subprocess supervisor per task instead, so each task has its own subreaper). Reap zombies. Add a test where a grandchild clears its env, calls setsid, its parent exits, and it keeps running: it must be killed and counted.
2. PID-reuse races. Bind identity with os.pidfd_open and signal.pidfd_send_signal for individual kills (open the pidfd, then re-verify starttime from /proc/<pid>/stat field 22, then signal via the fd). Do not killpg a group from an old snapshot: re-verify the group leader's identity, or signal members individually via pidfds. Tracked identities must be replaced, not setdefault'ed, when a pid's starttime changes. Never trust a bare root pid without its recorded starttime. Add tests for: reuse between check and signal (mocked), and a stale identity replaced.
3. A memory excess seen during cleanup must classify the task as a memory failure (the same status/fields as a live-scan memory kill), not ok. Apply the limit check to EVERY scan, including exit and cleanup scans. Regression test: 300 MiB limit, the parent exits 0, a background child is over the limit at cleanup -> memory failure, mem_kills>=1.
